{
  "description": "issue #686 (slice I7): the four handler forms COMPOSE. A named event `go`, a key `x`, a click, and an `every 1` all become due on the same tick (tick 1) during a `wait 1`; they fire in the normative same-tick order when -> on_key -> on_click -> due every (spec/interaction-events.md:144-149), so the bodies print 1, 2, 3, 4 in that exact order. The host input (executeOptions.hostInput) is supplied deliberately REVERSED (click, event, key) — the opposite of the normative kind order — so a run that inherited host-input order would print 3, 1, 2; printing 1, 2, 3, 4 proves the order is imposed at the drain point, not by the host. hostInput is host execution context (like signal): no tick, key, or coordinate appears in any event payload. JSON can only express a STATIC tick->deliveries schedule (like the already-cancelled signal), so input that reacts to program state stays a unit-test concern (packages/runtime/src/interaction-dispatch-order.test.mjs).",
